### 9. INTEGRATED MACHINE LEARNING — CORE ALGORITHM, NOT A PUBLIC-FACING FEATURE PAGE

The ML component must operate behind the main workflow. Do not create a large standalone AI classification page that distracts from the actual institutional monitoring objective.

The core algorithm should support:

1. Institution entity resolution where identifiers are missing or inconsistent.
2. Candidate related-complaint retrieval.
3. Semantic similarity between complaint descriptions.
4. Category or issue compatibility, when useful.
5. Prioritization of potential overlaps for human review.
6. Confidence and uncertainty reporting for authorized reviewers.

Use reliable deterministic rules first for exact institution-ID matching. Use fuzzy matching or an ML-assisted approach for uncertain identity matches and complaint relationships.

The algorithm must not treat text similarity alone as sufficient evidence that two cases are duplicates.

#### Recommended inference workflow

Incoming complaint
→ Institution identity resolution
→ Existing institution case retrieval
→ Candidate related-case comparison
→ Rule-based and/or ML-assisted relationship scoring
→ Potential overlap alert
→ Human review
→ Confirmed relationship or dismissed alert
→ Audit trail and coordinated action.

The user interface should show the result and operational meaning, rather than exposing the full technical algorithm to every user.

#### Officer-facing ML information

Authorized officers should be able to access a compact “Matching Evidence” or “Decision Support Details” panel.

Show:

- Matching institution identifiers or identity evidence.
- Relevant category or subject similarities.
- Related case IDs.
- Involved Bureaus/Cells.
- Current case statuses.
- Match confidence or similarity score, when available.
- Why the system raised the alert.
- What information is missing or uncertain.
- Whether a human reviewer has confirmed the relationship.

Do not show an arbitrary “94% AI accuracy” indicator for individual predictions.

#### Model performance and accuracy

Provide a restricted model-monitoring panel for authorized administrators and designated officers.

Display actual evaluation metrics only when they have been calculated from a documented evaluation dataset.

Depending on the implemented task, relevant metrics may include:

- Institution entity-matching precision and recall.
- Related-case detection precision and recall.
- F1 score.
- False-positive and false-negative counts.
- Human review confirmation rate.
- Performance by institution matching method or case category, when the sample size supports meaningful comparison.

Clearly distinguish offline model evaluation from live operational match confidence.

For example, “Evaluation F1: 0.87” is an evaluation result; it is not the same as “This pair has an 87% probability of being duplicates.”

If the real model or evaluation metrics are not connected, show “Model evaluation not configured” or “Demo matching mode.” Never invent accuracy or performance numbers.

